WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.150 --> 00:00:01.975
So what are the duties?

2
00:00:02.225 --> 00:00:05.725
Rule sixteen is the give-way vessel’s, and it’s short.

3
00:00:05.975 --> 00:00:09.875
Take early and substantial action to keep well clear.

4
00:00:10.125 --> 00:00:11.575
Early.

5
00:00:11.825 --> 00:00:13.550
Substantial.

6
00:00:13.800 --> 00:00:15.400
Well clear.

7
00:00:15.650 --> 00:00:20.750
Rule seventeen is the stand-on vessel’s, and it comes in three stages.

8
00:00:21.000 --> 00:00:22.625
Stage one.

9
00:00:22.875 --> 00:00:28.125
When risk of collision develops, the stand-on vessel shall keep her course and speed.

10
00:00:28.375 --> 00:00:30.350
That’s not stubbornness.

11
00:00:30.600 --> 00:00:33.975
It’s what lets the other captain predict what you’ll do.

12
00:00:34.225 --> 00:00:35.750
Stage two.

13
00:00:36.000 --> 00:00:46.025
As soon as it becomes apparent that the give-way vessel isn’t taking appropriate action, the stand-on vessel may act to avoid collision by her own maneuver alone.

14
00:00:46.275 --> 00:00:47.650
May.

15
00:00:47.900 --> 00:00:50.175
It’s allowed, not required.

16
00:00:50.425 --> 00:00:53.300
This is the moment for five short blasts.

17
00:00:53.550 --> 00:00:55.125
Stage three.

18
00:00:55.375 --> 00:01:04.525
When she’s so close that the give-way vessel alone can’t avoid the collision, the stand-on vessel shall take whatever action will best help avoid it.

19
00:01:04.775 --> 00:01:06.025
Shall.

20
00:01:06.275 --> 00:01:08.050
Now it’s required.

21
00:01:09.050 --> 00:01:12.575
So the pattern is: shall hold, may act, shall act.

22
00:01:12.825 --> 00:01:17.825
The exam tests the difference between may and shall, so lock that pattern in.
